 {:db/id "q-83144" :question/id #uuid "c60fbb60-b78a-5c24-90ef-165c91d4dd65" :question/math-academy-id "q-83144" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a video channel after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nIf the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, calculate the number of subscribers after $12$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.05S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the channel currently has $15\\,000$ subscribers, so we have ${S}_{0}=15\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of subscribers when $t=12,$ we substitute $t=12$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(12) & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.05\\cdot 12} \\\\  & =15\\,000\\,{e}^{0.6} \\\\  & ≈27\\,331.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of subscribers is $27\\,332$ after $12$ weeks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-83144/ma-source-field/c26c6b658e9288298b556ac71ee1975824dfd3d9a6dd32a2e03f9bdf65d476d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
