 {:db/id "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789" :answer-field/id #uuid "adc9838b-0769-5bf4-b3d7-c8c2300681dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/074db705e16e1e57f8a2aff94b5fe57d6f39b7233e5e76f422f3f665da482360" "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/171af9e1c9f732f0a40cb09e06b917bfb1d83ecb667e2e6fc9149e02322838e3" "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/412b9ee8422a2c1cd2c9d97e8a0a65bdb10407afd20caaa31a1ca12e83a832db" "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/0e217de0f562a2169cf62f92e096d4956b843916c9efa189b0ce9e6dff99e8fa" "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/de0e2ca3bccfd81ed52dc353be6df152df6ee2e05a6b29884be5e2b0ea2076e8"] :answer-field/correct "q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789/074db705e16e1e57f8a2aff94b5fe57d6f39b7233e5e76f422f3f665da482360" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-83480" :question/id #uuid "ae635709-cef0-5d90-bcf8-39aaac1bc474" :question/math-academy-id "q-83480" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{9}{5}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83480/ma-source-field/c777ba20cdd61eba9774ee49249bc7991b1d451a04711bb0e4daa06c34f2a789"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
