 {:db/id "q-83826" :question/id #uuid "6ee6c969-d319-57a0-b1a1-3435c9110a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-83826" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x+6?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }7}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-7$ does not take the above form. The value $-7$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{7}{1},$ and the numerator $(7)$ is not a factor of $6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83826/ma-source-field/48feb339505fcbc26b2254e5b2458c8f5f03ff0c485d754698e603148c941d8e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
