 {:db/id "q-84598" :question/id #uuid "2e3e5aa9-3c15-5f62-9549-6ee08ad4a6f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84598" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-84598/ma-source-field/00fc353648a53f69474e9590622ad472210e973b22be2430200704eb4718ce90"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
