 {:db/id "q-85966/ma-source-field/02147363dd5165963b01de568d88fcc9752058bba98457830e91392c037aa5fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "c119d922-a10c-5cfb-9f5d-697a1f5368d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-85966/ma-source-field/02147363dd5165963b01de568d88fcc9752058bba98457830e91392c037aa5fc/05ac00bcc16d4b2d08ac9ab18e656edb2fa47a5aa8f633fe0f5a56559828843a" "q-85966/ma-source-field/02147363dd5165963b01de568d88fcc9752058bba98457830e91392c037aa5fc/8f9ed2a91396b3e44a408bb97d7a3017a9918df3c39ce46a18b808c7085844e8" "q-85966/ma-source-field/02147363dd5165963b01de568d88fcc9752058bba98457830e91392c037aa5fc/b0753e03decacc5945bd19d5138d477f578068100b51402d3fbfad4be36d087d" "q-85966/ma-source-field/02147363dd5165963b01de568d88fcc9752058bba98457830e91392c037aa5fc/78b0990d1ebcf3679ab040ab2b15a98499b03069551bfa6d44d1656e9dc76fad" "q-85966/ma-source-field/02147363dd5165963b01de568d88fcc9752058bba98457830e91392c037aa5fc/a0715085b8cd9d31e7e81fd4fc47e4617263f8c98c2689afc5604b3b9993464c"] :answer-field/correct "q-85966/ma-source-field/02147363dd5165963b01de568d88fcc9752058bba98457830e91392c037aa5fc/a0715085b8cd9d31e7e81fd4fc47e4617263f8c98c2689afc5604b3b9993464c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-85966" :question/id #uuid "179e9be2-d5dc-5bf7-a08f-f77d3bd121bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-85966" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8e03226558b1dbb2e93d23947b0b05f3d8413dc35207bbe9b536e68108f40955.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ while the $5\\,\\text{cm}$ height does not change. If the volume of the rectangular prism is increasing at a rate of $15\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $6\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $2\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =2\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =15\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=6\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $z$ is not changing and $z=5,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=5xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5xy) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =5x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+5y(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =5(6)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+5(2)(-3) \\\\ 15 & =30(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})-30 \\\\ 45 & =30(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=6\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-85966/ma-source-field/02147363dd5165963b01de568d88fcc9752058bba98457830e91392c037aa5fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
