 {:db/id "q-89589/ma-source-field/54e4dc9b53794e6bbde2c433e7d53a284e4e1aa4c21c2f3a9e3ca85fafbec135" :answer-field/id #uuid "b29f83ae-6ce7-5677-8e73-4e7762c39fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-89589/ma-source-field/54e4dc9b53794e6bbde2c433e7d53a284e4e1aa4c21c2f3a9e3ca85fafbec135/76e98a77085f276070fce0f19a57521b91e5e35c504d4322a9778065298c7f4a" "q-89589/ma-source-field/54e4dc9b53794e6bbde2c433e7d53a284e4e1aa4c21c2f3a9e3ca85fafbec135/55b3ac8687427dfb5b75397138a575971471a4b41449f1562c111fcb2459e7eb" "q-89589/ma-source-field/54e4dc9b53794e6bbde2c433e7d53a284e4e1aa4c21c2f3a9e3ca85fafbec135/aae36ca6a1f1b5255a99035980bcd1495d2b8fcf3efd7f0c2b0e42d6861b7a2a" "q-89589/ma-source-field/54e4dc9b53794e6bbde2c433e7d53a284e4e1aa4c21c2f3a9e3ca85fafbec135/d2d3993b23a7c5a90d56873714b84c15d6c842e716ec63d8e15cc14b24fb0ddd" "q-89589/ma-source-field/54e4dc9b53794e6bbde2c433e7d53a284e4e1aa4c21c2f3a9e3ca85fafbec135/27fd4dd787eaf295db46ae1d5af5eaa525fe20d528090e3f5cc422ecfe418b66"] :answer-field/correct "q-89589/ma-source-field/54e4dc9b53794e6bbde2c433e7d53a284e4e1aa4c21c2f3a9e3ca85fafbec135/55b3ac8687427dfb5b75397138a575971471a4b41449f1562c111fcb2459e7eb" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "5620868a-e836-565c-a7cc-7e2d887588f3"] :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{4t}} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{-{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2 \\\\ L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}-L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)-(s+2)}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{(s+2)(s-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{(s+2)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{-2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-2,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (4,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-2,\\infty )\\cap (4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-89589/ma-source-field/54e4dc9b53794e6bbde2c433e7d53a284e4e1aa4c21c2f3a9e3ca85fafbec135"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
