 {:db/id "q-94267" :question/id #uuid "873cc1aa-ff8b-506b-b81a-49f440b9d6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94267" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents SHM is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nIn the above case:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94267/ma-source-field/f2abfd8c0cd7ce7e438b9319cd887998eea908ac2f80474a561dbc684e8cc41a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
