 {:db/id "q-94283" :question/id #uuid "3cf6d9af-b061-5993-b553-d544e56a866d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94283" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2\\sqrt{2}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=2\\sqrt{2}R\\,⟹\\,R=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94283/ma-source-field/6f304c73f19ec3f75c0f4bded21f2c4f058be8f76f6d61d8db0e07da47da8fbd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
