 {:db/id "q-94609" :question/id #uuid "8ed2d339-1669-5c85-b00e-540acdbbec95" :question/math-academy-id "q-94609" :question/problem "Find the center of the elliptic cone $4(x-1)^{2}+10(y-4)^{2}={z}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}+10(y-4)^{2} & =(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{1/10} & =(z-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(1,4,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94609/ma-source-field/7c3d1af2662a8686d500207febe06092bfdcfbd03b2e52ed5a2f7ca79194d9ef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
