 {:db/id "q-95216" :question/id #uuid "20513819-0985-5cdd-a8ca-960c927c6ced" :question/math-academy-id "q-95216" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 4$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R))=6\\cdot 4=24.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-95216/ma-source-field/48a7173c34e06d46724806bd64a495489be35819ffdf359a0b0b785fb7ab131e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
