 {:db/id "q-97071" :question/id #uuid "fdcf8d93-f073-5ef6-b0fa-29dd34699bc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97071" :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(6,2)$ and $(9,3),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{3-2}{9-6}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-97071/ma-source-field/8bb7a0d86d8cc50ff1a7d9a224e205bc19a21b6e7570b8bc6522aaedc774afdc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
