 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "9a668edb-9a8d-5acd-b6b7-2627f5a72df0"] :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨3,2,5⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4,1⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=2$ and $f(b)=3,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 3-2 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4,1⟩-⟨3,2,5⟩) \\\\ 1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ 1 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-4{f}_{z}(c) \\\\ -1 & =2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c)=-1.$ *" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-98117/ma-source-field/7f21bd9f7f303846536645ba71b74e094dce33418a7f5b6dfb1d92bfa9e8bf2b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
