 {:db/id "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8" :answer-field/id #uuid "76d542a7-e1e6-5040-8811-6896030adc94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/125323c1578fa12db3ef9f2f56c96b77b6f9c052aea0c24629400db3f5b1bf8e" "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/8703ffd5f81c67de754180beb16bc00f9a5b83cba1a8c98f77aa5e3839ac5bd6" "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/ab5c1ecd9ac0bb9558a714e926006ab82e40522cbc25ed0aae8752f1be1f4edb" "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/2ca6dfeda5c06ea7ec223ee5cb0b022f1345410f74719679c91e546423ab2ebe" "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/7d7311cccb2fd2ea842062fde00339b4d4f6d9c6561c0fe19cd3746cfe6a0367"] :answer-field/correct "q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8/125323c1578fa12db3ef9f2f56c96b77b6f9c052aea0c24629400db3f5b1bf8e" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-99009" :question/id #uuid "99338d6e-762d-5e46-924e-c9b8ff055bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-99009" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99009/ma-source-field/fa58b6befc4c6088d0eaef4573ca4cbf0aa0aae95957368781391fd4921962f8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
