 {:db/id "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e" :answer-field/id #uuid "25347463-2d58-522d-8979-b8544d1335f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/a835e9a19410f9ea67ea9774df070473e15060f59f33a9cfad9eb5d178f9e638" "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/ae88b0e4e39efaf2d30ebd55cc98cb3ed5733c3f224b932ff48a4224b22ca5a1" "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935" "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/d144965a68a71154d2438737e51156d9918da199ac2f07b96e885a6c60965580" "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/f54ee287f31559da0166b1ce79ed1a7447405825f846c727aac2c733e9b87b3e"] :answer-field/correct "q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e/bd6a8fadee21d3bae00ca3beb5bb88107d613102577da4647e80fb7b41fbe935" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-99204" :question/id #uuid "8e5f2d5d-b1b9-503b-9dbd-36de49acff81" :question/math-academy-id "q-99204" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum product of three positive numbers given that the sum of their squares is $108.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=108.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=108\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}yz=2\\lambda x \\\\ xz=2\\lambda y \\\\ xy=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}xyz=2\\lambda {x}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {y}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(6,6,6),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(6,6,6)=6\\cdot 6\\cdot 6=216.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,2,2) & =10\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & <f(6,6,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99204/ma-source-field/8ed647979d1e6c14bc72e17cb37d81fe9c2b89e716dae1d839d489a497bdc18e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
